---
// Lesson 10.1c — 3-Button Jam: Core Loop & Self-Playtest
// Close the start -> play -> win/lose -> 1-button retry loop with one difficulty ramp,
// then put the game through a structured 5-test Self-Playtest before classmates ever see it.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>10.1c — 3-Button Jam: Core Loop &amp; Self-Playtest</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    A toy becomes a <strong>game</strong> the moment it can start cleanly, escalate tension, end in
    a clear win or loss, and restart in one button press. Once your loop closes today, you put
    on your cruelest critic hat and run a five-part <strong>Self-Playtest</strong> to expose edge
    cases before friends ever touch the keyboard.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code + Editor: Close the Loop &amp; Add Escalation (~30 min)</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li><strong>Track Score or Progress On Screen:</strong> wire a simple TextMeshProUGUI readout for score, timer, or health so players never guess how close they are to winning or losing.</li>
        <li><strong>Build One Clear Escalation Ramp:</strong> avoid building five static stages when one dynamic ramp creates real tension — speed up spawns over time, shrink a timer using <code>Mathf.Max()</code>, or introduce a second hazard shape once score passes a threshold.</li>
        <li><strong>Wire Instant Restart Inside the 3 Buttons:</strong> when Game Over or Victory triggers, display a short message (e.g., <code>"Press J to Play Again"</code>) calling <code>SceneManager.LoadScene()</code> so restarting takes half a second with zero mouse clicking.</li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Structured Self-Playtest: Try to Break Your Own Game (~25 min)</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Developers usually play gently along the "intended path." Force yourself through these five
        adversarial tests and write down every failure honestly:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Self-Test</th><th>What You Intentionally Do</th><th>Pass / What Broke?</th></tr>
        <tr><td>1. Idle Test</td><td>Touch ZERO keys for 15 seconds after starting.</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>2. Spam Test</td><td>Mash all 3 buttons as fast as possible simultaneously.</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>3. Edge / Hold Test</td><td>Hold movement/charge keys permanently against screen edges or boundaries.</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>4. 3-Button Audit</td><td>Play 3 full runs (Start → Die/Win → Retry) WITHOUT touching any 4th key or mouse outside your 3 buttons.</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>5. Escalation Curve</td><td>Play seriously for 60 seconds: is the start boring or instantly impossible, and does the difficulty ramp noticeably?</td><td></td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Catching Your Own Blind Spots</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Which of the five self-tests broke or embarrassed your game most today (idle survival, button mashing, escaping off-screen, or restart state leaking across runs)?</li>
            <li>Compare playing your own game cleanly versus running the Spam and Idle tests: why do edge-case tests catch bugs that normal playthroughs hide for days?</li>
            <li>Name the single fix you will ship BEFORE friend playtesting next class so testers actually spend time reacting to your design rather than getting trapped in a bug.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Complete a full start → challenge → escalation → win/loss → instant retry loop</li>
        <li>Keep score/health/goal visible on a TextMeshProUGUI HUD</li>
        <li>Run all five self-playtest stress tests and fix soft-locks or off-screen escapes</li>
        <li>Commit and push your self-tested loop cleanly</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/index.html">Unit 10: Jam Season</a> — core loop &amp; self-playtesting</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — <code>SceneManager.LoadScene()</code>, TextMeshPro, and cooldown ramps</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="10.1d.html">10.1d — 3-Button Jam: Friend Playtest &amp; Triage</a> — put strangers in front of your three buttons with zero coaching.</p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="10.1c" title="10.1c 3-Button Jam: Core Loop &amp; Self-Playtest — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
